1. Introduction

Implicit conversion happens when the C++ compiler automatically changes a value from one data type to another without being told. It makes programming easier, but sometimes it can produce unexpected results.

In the metro fare system, conversions must be handled carefully because even a small mistake can affect the fare and balance calculations.

2. Types of Implicit Conversions

a) Integer Division

When two integer values are divided, C++ gives an integer result. The decimal part is removed.

Example:
int a = 7;
int b = 2;
double c = a / b;

Here, the result is 3, not 3.5, because integer division takes place first.

To get the correct decimal result, we can use:

double c = static_cast<double>(a) / b;

Now the result will be 3.5.

b) Integer to Double Conversion

When an integer value is stored in a double variable, C++ automatically converts it into a double.
int x = 10;
double y = x;

Here, the value 10 is automatically converted into 10.0.

c) Signed and Unsigned Comparison

A signed integer can store both negative and positive values, whereas an unsigned integer can store only zero and positive values.
int a = -1;
unsigned int b = 1;


When these values are compared, C++ converts the signed value into an unsigned value. This can produce an unexpected result. The compiler may also show a warning when signed and unsigned values are compared.

d) Narrowing Conversion

Narrowing happens when a value is converted into a data type that cannot safely store the original value.

Example:
int x = 3.7;
Here, the decimal part is removed, and the value becomes 3.

Brace initialisation prevents this type of conversion.
int x{3.7};


This statement produces a compilation error because it tries to store a decimal value in an integer variable.

e) Use of static_cast

static_cast is used when we want to convert a value into another data type explicitly.

Example:
int a = 7;
double b = static_cast<double>(a) / 2;


Here, the integer is converted into a double before division. This gives the correct decimal result.

