6. Correcting the Conversion Problems

To avoid unexpected conversions, I can make the conversions clear in the code.

Integer division:
double result = static_cast<double>(7) / 2;


This gives 3.5 instead of 3.

Narrowing conversion:
double x{3.7};


This safely stores the decimal value. If I want to convert it into an integer, I can use:
int x = static_cast<int>(3.7);


This gives 3, but the decimal part is lost.

Signed and unsigned comparison:
int s = -1;
unsigned int u = 1;

if (s < 0)
{
    cout << "s is negative";
}
else
{
    cout << "s is not negative";
}


Checking the negative value separately avoids an unexpected signed and unsigned comparison.

7. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

Instead of allowing the compiler to perform conversions automatically, I can use static_cast wherever a conversion is needed and brace initialisation when declaring variables.

For example:
int a{7};
int b{2};

double result = static_cast<double>(a) / b;


This makes the conversion clear and avoids accidental integer division.
- Time Complexity: Both implicit and explicit conversions take O(1) time for each conversion.
- Space Complexity: Both methods use O(1) extra space. Explicit conversion does not normally require additional memory.
- Compile-time Errors: Brace initialisation prevents narrowing conversions by producing a compilation error.
- Accuracy: Explicit conversions make the programmer's intention clear and help avoid unexpected results.
Therefore, using explicit conversions makes the program easier to understand and reduces conversion-related mistakes without changing the overall time or space complexity.

